  "assumptions": [
    "Illustration: a made-up two-setting loss bowl with simulated batch noise, not a real model trained on real data.",
    "Loss = (3x² + z²)/2, drawn as a surface at half its value. It is 3 times steeper left-right, so large rates overshoot there first; above a rate of 2/3 the left-right swings grow.",
    "Each step = −rate × (true slope + noise). Noise is random with sd 2·√((64−b)/(63·b)) for batch b: 2 at b=1, exactly 0 at b=64. A step lowers loss only on average.",
    "Orange arrow: the mini-batch step about to be taken; grey: the step the full-data slope would give. Both are drawn at 2× their true length.",
    "While playing, one update happens every 6 host steps (5 per second). A run ends after 40 updates (finished), or as diverged when the loss passes 6.",
    "Noise comes from a fixed seed; Reset replays the same sequence. A new run uses the next noise values. Diverged positions are capped at the bowl's edge on screen; the loss readout stays true.",
    "Trails hold the start point plus up to 40 landing points."
  ]
}